   "explained_by_fundamentals": false,
   "explanation": "On 9 November 2026, the 16:00–20:00 day-ahead price averaged **553.29 EUR/MWh**, against a mean of 270.39 EUR/MWh for the same clock hours on 20 comparable weekdays (12 Oct–8 Nov 2026). That is 282.9 EUR/MWh (+104.6 %) higher, a z-score of 3.9.\n\n- **Low wind and no solar pushed up residual load.** Residual load (demand minus wind and solar) was 63.3 GW, 51 % above the comparable-day mean of 41.9 GW. Wind averaged 4.4 GW against 18.5 GW, and solar 68 MW against 2,067 MW. Demand was also higher, at 67.7 GW against 62.5 GW (+8.4 %). Station wind (DWD, about 10 m, a 65-station average) was 3.0 m/s against 3.6 m/s, so it is only an indicator.\n- **The merit-order estimate expects 360.75 EUR/MWh,** leaving about 192.5 EUR/MWh unexplained. The event's residual load was above the highest level in the fit (60.1 GW), where the real merit order is much steeper. The unexplained part is therefore most likely scarcity pricing, though gas and CO2 prices, plant outages and bids are not in the data and cannot be ruled out.\n- **The gap was covered by gas, lignite and imports.** Gas generation was 18.6 GW (+73.5 %), lignite 13.4 GW (+18.4 %), and net imports 8.0 GW against 1.8 GW. These responses reflect how the gap was filled, not necessarily why the price rose.\n\nThese are associations over four weeks, not proof of cause.\n\nSource: Bundesnetzagentur | SMARD.de via Energy-Charts.info (Fraunhofer ISE) for prices and generation; DWD for weather.",
   "explanation_steps": [
    "Compared the price on 9 Nov 2026, 16:00–20:00, with the same hours on comparable days of the 4 weeks before, and estimated the price residual load would normally give"
